@@s001 map2, sequence, traverse
@@s002 ora:element combined now,ok:accumulator Some,no:accumulator None
@@s003 <code>sequence(List(Some(1), None, Some(3)))</code> with foldRight: one starts from the bottom with <code>Some(Nil)</code>, "all ok, no value".
@@s004 Last element: <code>map2(Some(3), Some(Nil))(_ :: _)</code> = <b>Some(List(3))</b>.
@@s005 Middle element: <code>map2(None, Some(List(3)))</code>. A value is missing: the result is <b>None</b>.
@@s006 First element: 1 is there, but the accumulator is None, and map2 with a None gives None.
@@s007 Result <b>None</b>: one failure is enough for everything to fail. With List(Some(1), Some(2), Some(3)) it would be Some(List(1, 2, 3)) (checked).
@@s008 dato:first Option,due:second Option,ok:result,no:None
@@s009 ora:element added,dato:list already built
@@s010 ora:element,ok:Some,no:None
@@s011 map2, sequence, traverse
@@s012 Exercises 9–11. Three ways of <b>combining several Options</b>: <code>map2</code> joins two, <code>sequence</code> turns a list of Options into an Option of a list, <code>traverse</code> does <code>map</code> and <code>sequence</code> together. In all of them: if even a single piece is missing, the result is <code>None</code>.
@@s013 <code>map2</code>: combining two Options
@@s014 Exercise 9
@@s015 Two values that may be there, and an ordinary function that combines them.
@@s016 I open the first Option: if there is one, I call its value <code>a</code>. I use <code>flatMap</code> because what I do next still returns an Option.
@@s017 I open the second: if there is one, I call it <code>b</code>. Here <code>map</code> is enough, because <code>f</code> gives an ordinary value.
@@s018 With both in hand, I combine. If one of the two was <code>None</code>, <code>f</code> is never called.
@@s019 Two runs
@@s020 Both present.
@@s021 Both are opened.
@@s022 Checked.
@@s023 The second is missing: <code>map</code> on None gives None, and that becomes the result.
@@s024 Two ways of seeing it
@@s025 1) <code>map2</code> "lifts" a two-argument function into the world of Options. 2) It is a <b>sequencer</b>: it runs two calculations that can fail and combines the results only if both succeed.
@@s026 Open two Options, combine them if they are there
@@s027 The symbol <code>::</code>
@@s028 From week 3 the lists of the standard library are used. <code>::</code> is their <code>Cons</code>, written in the middle: <code>x :: resto</code> is the list with <code>x</code> in front of <code>resto</code>.
@@s029 1 :: 2 :: 3 :: Nil
@@s030 I start from the empty list.
@@s031 I put 2 in front.
@@s032 With the placeholders it is a function of two arguments: an element and a list. We will use it in a moment.
@@s033 Week 2 (course list)
@@s034 From week 3 (library)
@@s035 <code>Cons(1, Cons(2, Nil))</code>
@@s036 <code>1 :: 2 :: Nil</code>
@@s037 <code>case Cons(h, t) =></code>
@@s038 <code>case h :: t =></code>
@@s039 Building List(1, 2, 3) from the right
@@s040 <code>sequence</code>: from List[Option[A]] to Option[List[A]]
@@s041 Example from the text: for each employee you have asked the database for the salary. Each answer is an Option (None if the connection dropped). You want <b>all</b> the salaries, or to know that something went wrong.
@@s042 Exercise 10
@@s043 Inside and outside swap places: a list of "maybes" becomes a "maybe" of a list.
@@s044 I reduce the list with <code>foldRight</code>. The result is an <code>Option[List[A]]</code>: I write it in square brackets to help the compiler.
@@s045 Initial value: an empty list of Options becomes <code>Some(Nil)</code>, "all ok, no value". Not <code>None</code>: on zero requests nothing has failed.
@@s046 Step: I combine the current Option <code>ao</code> with the Option-list already built <code>acc</code>. If both are there, I put the value in front of the list with <code>::</code>.
@@s047 sequence(List(Some(1), None, Some(3)))
@@s048 foldRight starts from the bottom.
@@s049 Last element: present.
@@s050 The second is missing: from here the accumulator is None.
@@s051 Even though 1 is there, the accumulator is None.
@@s052 Results checked.
@@s053 The accumulator of sequence
@@s054 Where it goes
@@s055 The text observes that <code>sequence</code> should not be a method of <code>List</code> (it would tie lists to Options) and cannot be a method of <code>Option</code> (its argument is a list). So it goes as a function of its own, in the file or in the companion object.
@@s056 <code>traverse</code>: map and sequence in one pass
@@s057 Exercise 11
@@s058 Like <code>map</code>, but the function can fail.
@@s059 Same structure as <code>sequence</code>: the only difference is <code>f(a)</code> in place of <code>ao</code>. I apply <code>f</code> while I go through.
@@s060 <b>Why not <code>sequence(as.map(f))</code>?</b> It gives the same result, but <code>map</code> knows nothing about Options: it applies <code>f</code> to the <b>whole</b> list and builds an intermediate list, even if the first element fails. <code>traverse</code> does everything in a single pass.
@@s061 map that can fail, in one pass
